  "reasoning": "The patient presents with high fever and persistent shivering, indicating an acute systemic febrile illness. The concurrent presence of rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck strongly points toward a viral exanthem or vector-borne illness. Among the provided options, Dengue classically presents with sudden high fever, chills/shivering, and a characteristic maculopapular rash that frequently appears on the extremities and spreads to the trunk/neck. Malaria shares the fever and chills but typically lacks a prominent rash. Drug reactions and allergies cause rashes but generally do not present with high fever and pronounced shivering. Impetigo involves sores near the nose rather than generalized arm/neck patches. Therefore, the combination of high fever, shivering, and specific rash distribution establishes Dengue as the correct diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
